<task id="046" project="unified-access-control-r2">
  <metadata>
    <title>Create the Secure Project Owner security role, assign it to the Secure Project owner team, and REMOVE System Administrator from that team</title>
    <phase>0b Review remediation</phase>
    <status>completed</status>
    <rigor>FULL</rigor>
    <rigor-reason>Dataverse security-role configuration on the business unit that the entire Secure Project isolation model depends on. Getting the depth wrong either breaks ownership assignment (provisioning fails closed) or over-grants on the BU whose reachability NFR-05 exists to guard.</rigor-reason>
    <model-tier>opus</model-tier>
    <model-tier-reason>Dataverse privilege-depth semantics for TEAM principals are subtle and are the exact thing this project has repeatedly got wrong by assumption; the failure mode is silent over-grant.</model-tier-reason>
    <effort>xhigh</effort>
    <effort-reason>Requires empirical determination against live Dataverse (which privileges an assignment target actually needs, at which depth) rather than implementing a documented answer -- the documented answer is what needs testing.</effort-reason>
    <parallel-group>none</parallel-group>
    <parallel-safe>false</parallel-safe>
    <parallel-reason>Live Dataverse security configuration; must not run concurrently with anything that provisions or reads secure projects.</parallel-reason>
    <deps>021</deps>
    <tags>dataverse, auth, security</tags>
    <estimated-effort>3-5 hours</estimated-effort>
    <origin>Filed 2026-08-25 by owner decision, from task 021's live-environment finding: the `Secure Project` default owner team exists and is correctly memberless, but the ONLY role on it is System Administrator, and no role matching `Secure%` exists in the environment. Review §D says of this exact question: "None -- and definitely NOT System Administrator."</origin>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>The Secure Project owner team currently holds System Administrator. Create a dedicated `Secure Project Owner` role carrying the minimum privileges Dataverse actually requires for that team to own secure records, assign it to that team only, remove System Administrator, and prove by experiment that ownership assignment still succeeds and that no human principal gains anything.</prompt>

  <role>SPAARKE Dataverse security administrator. Expert in security roles, privilege depths, owner teams, and the difference between what a principal needs to OWN a record and what it needs to ACT on one.</role>

  <goal>A `Secure Project Owner` role exists, holds the least privilege that empirically works, is assigned to the `Secure Project` default owner team and to nothing else; System Administrator is removed from that team; the team remains memberless; provisioning's ownership assignment still succeeds; and NFR-05's assertion can be stated truthfully.</goal>

  <context>
    <background>LIVE STATE, verified 2026-08-25 (task 021):

| Fact | Value |
|---|---|
| BU | `Secure Project` — `d9ec0b6f-80a0-f111-aaac-000d3a99d1d7`, parented to root `Spaarke`, created 2026-08-25 08:28 |
| Default owner team | `Secure Project` — `daec0b6f-80a0-f111-aaac-000d3a99d1d7`, teamtype=0 (Owner), isdefault=Yes |
| Team members | ZERO (correct per design 5.1a) |
| Team roles | EXACTLY ONE: `System Administrator` (roleid 3980a53d-b0cf-3ded-37c8-4d4f9b94acef) |
| Roles named `Secure%` | NONE EXIST |
| Secure projects in the environment | ZERO — none has ever been provisioned |

Nothing is exposed today because the team has no members. But the posture is ONE membership row away from granting a human full administrative rights, on the business unit whose reachability NFR-05 exists to guard. That is the inverse of the intent.

WHY THIS BLOCKS TRUSTING PROVISIONING. Task 021 assigns each secure project to this team, and it carries an escalation trigger for "assignment failed because the team lacks entity privileges". That trigger CANNOT FIRE while the team is System Administrator — assignment succeeds because the team is omnipotent, not because it is correctly scoped. So a green provisioning run in dev currently proves nothing about the role. This task is what makes that signal meaningful.

THE PRIVILEGE QUESTION IS OPEN, NOT SETTLED. design 5.1a specifies "Create / Read / Write / Delete / Append / AppendTo / Share on sprk_project and its child entities, at Business Unit depth." Treat that as a HYPOTHESIS to test, not a spec to implement. Two things in it deserve challenge:

(1) DEPTH. For a TEAM principal, User/Basic depth means "records the team owns". Since this team owns exactly the secure projects, USER depth may be sufficient — and it is strictly tighter than Business Unit depth. BU depth is also precisely what made NFR-05's original assertion false by construction. START AT USER DEPTH and escalate only where something empirically fails. If User depth works, say so and amend design 5.1a and spec NFR-05 accordingly — the amendment becomes narrower, which is a better outcome than the one currently written down.

(2) WHICH PRIVILEGES. Dataverse requires an assignment TARGET to hold the privileges needed to own the record — in practice Read and Write; Create is arguably not needed because the team never creates (the BFF app user creates, then assigns). Determine this EMPIRICALLY: grant the minimum, attempt an assignment, add only what Dataverse actually refuses without. Do not grant a privilege because a doc listed it.

CHILD ENTITIES — SCOPE THIS DELIBERATELY, DO NOT ASSUME. Task 021 assigns ONLY the sprk_project record. Whether child records (documents, invoices, work assignments, communications, events, to-dos, analyses) should ALSO be team-owned is a genuine design question with a security consequence: a document on a secure project that is owned in the Operations BU is readable there. Known child entities that roll up to a project, per `ExternalModuleRegistry`: `sprk_document` (via sprk_document.sprk_project), `sprk_invoice` (sprk_invoice.sprk_project), `sprk_workassignment` (sprk_regardingproject). Determine the full set by querying which entities carry a lookup to sprk_project. If the answer is "children should be team-owned too", that is a change to task 021's behaviour and belongs in its own task — surface it, do not implement it here.

WHAT MUST REMAIN TRUE AFTERWARDS:
- The team has NO human members. The role's whole safety argument rests on this.
- The role is assigned to that ONE team and to no user and no other team.
- Ordinary user roles KEEP Read on sprk_project at Basic/User depth. A POA share is only effective if the user also holds the entity privilege at some depth — stripping it in the name of "securing" the entity silently disables sharing altogether (design 5.1a).</background>
