      <file role="new">docs/guides/SECURE-PROJECT-ENVIRONMENT-SETUP.md</file>
      <file role="reference">projects/unified-access-control-r2/notes/secure-project-workflow-review-2026-08-24.md</file>
    </relevant-files>
  </context>

  <constraints>
    <constraint source="project">LEAST PRIVILEGE, DETERMINED EMPIRICALLY. Start from the smallest plausible grant and add only what Dataverse demonstrably refuses without. Record each addition WITH the error that forced it. A role built by copying a list from a document is exactly how this project accumulated eight stale-metadata findings.</constraint>
    <constraint source="design 5.1a">The team MUST remain free of human members, and this must be re-verified after the change. The role's BU/User depth is only safe because nobody holds it through membership.</constraint>
    <constraint source="design 5.1a">DO NOT strip `sprk_project` Read from ordinary user roles. A share confers nothing unless the user also holds the entity privilege at some depth; removing it would silently disable all sharing, including FR-28's.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">VERIFY AGAINST LIVE METADATA, not schema docs. Eight stale-doc instances have been found in this project; the eighth was the `Secure Project` business unit name itself, which every doc had as plural. Read the environment.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">This is environment configuration, and dev is the only live environment. Every change must be reproducible on a fresh environment — capture it as a runbook, not as a sequence of clicks somebody performed once.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-003">If the role turns out to be insufficient for provisioning, provisioning must FAIL CLOSED (it does — task 021 returns a non-2xx with `sdap.provision.owner_assignment_failed`). Do not widen the role to make a failure disappear without understanding what it was telling you.</constraint>
  </constraints>

  <knowledge>
    <files>
      <file>projects/unified-access-control-r2/design.md</file>
      <file>projects/unified-access-control-r2/notes/secure-project-workflow-review-2026-08-24.md</file>
      <file>projects/unified-access-control-r2/notes/task-021-provisioning-stamping.md</file>
      <file>projects/unified-access-control-r2/spec.md</file>
    </files>
  </knowledge>

  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="0">Re-read the live state (BU, team, team roles, team members) and confirm it still matches the table above. Enumerate the entities carrying a lookup to `sprk_project` so the child-entity question is answered from metadata rather than memory.</step>
    <step order="1">Create the `Secure Project Owner` role in the `Secure Project` business unit. Start with Read + Write on `sprk_project` at USER depth and nothing else.</step>
    <step order="2">Assign the role to the `Secure Project` default owner team. Do NOT yet remove System Administrator — keep the working configuration until the new one is proven.</step>
    <step order="3">EXPERIMENT: create a test `sprk_project` with `sprk_issecure = true` and attempt to assign its owner to the team. Record exactly what Dataverse does. Add one privilege at a time ONLY when an operation fails, recording the error that forced each addition.</step>
    <step order="4">Once assignment succeeds on the minimum set, REMOVE System Administrator from the team and re-run the experiment. This is the step that proves the new role — not the previous one.</step>
    <step order="5">Verify no human reachability: the team still has zero members; the role is on no user and no other team; and an ordinary non-admin user still cannot read the test secure project.</step>
    <step order="6">Verify ordinary sharing still works: share the test project with a non-admin user and confirm they can read it — this is what proves Basic/User Read was not stripped, and it is FR-28's prerequisite.</step>
    <step order="7">Answer the child-entity question from step 0's metadata and state a recommendation. If children should be team-owned, file it as a separate task against task 021's assignment behaviour rather than implementing it here.</step>
    <step order="8">Write the runbook: `docs/guides/SECURE-PROJECT-ENVIRONMENT-SETUP.md` — the exact reproducible steps for a new environment (BU, owner team, this role, what must NOT be granted, and the verification checks). Record the final privilege set WITH the reason each entry survived.</step>
    <step order="9">Amend design 5.1a and spec NFR-05 to the depth that actually proved necessary. If User depth sufficed, NFR-05's exemption narrows — say so explicitly, since the current amendment was written assuming BU depth.</step>
    <step order="10">Delete the test project and any test artifacts. Update notes, TASK-INDEX, current-task.md.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>If ownership assignment requires a privilege that would make the team able to read records OUTSIDE the Secure Project business unit, STOP and escalate. That would mean the ownership model itself needs revisiting, not that the role needs widening.</trigger>
    <trigger>If removing System Administrator breaks something other than the assignment (e.g. an unrelated integration was silently relying on that team's rights), STOP and report what depended on it — a memberless team should have no dependents, and one appearing is a finding.</trigger>
    <trigger>If the child-entity analysis shows that documents on a secure project are currently owned in a business unit ordinary users can reach, STOP and escalate immediately — that is a live disclosure, not a configuration preference, and it outranks this task.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <justification>
    <existing>The `Secure Project` business unit and its default owner team already exist. No new team, and no service account (design 5.1a establishes none is needed).</existing>
    <extension>One new security role — which cannot be an extension of an existing one: every existing role is either System Administrator (far too broad) or a general user role that must NOT reach this business unit.</extension>
    <cost-of-doing-nothing>The team that owns every secure project holds System Administrator, so adding one member would grant that person full administrative rights over the whole organisation — and the team is the ownership anchor of the entire isolation model. Separately, task 021's escalation trigger for "the team lacks privileges" cannot fire while the team is omnipotent, so provisioning cannot be meaningfully verified in dev at all.</cost-of-doing-nothing>
  </justification>

  <outputs>
    <output type="config">Dataverse: `Secure Project Owner` security role, assigned to the Secure Project default owner team</output>
    <output type="doc">docs/guides/SECURE-PROJECT-ENVIRONMENT-SETUP.md</output>
    <output type="spec">projects/unified-access-control-r2/spec.md</output>
    <output type="doc">projects/unified-access-control-r2/design.md</output>
  </outputs>

  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion>A `Secure Project Owner` role exists in the `Secure Project` business unit and is assigned to that BU's default owner team.</criterion>
    <criterion>`System Administrator` is NO LONGER on that team, and assignment still succeeds afterwards — proven by re-running the experiment after removal, not before.</criterion>
    <criterion>Every privilege in the role is justified by a recorded failure that occurred without it.</criterion>
    <criterion>The team has zero human members; the role is held by no user and no other team.</criterion>
    <criterion>A non-admin user cannot read a secure project by any path other than an explicit share.</criterion>
    <criterion>A shared non-admin user CAN read it — proving Basic/User Read was not stripped.</criterion>
    <criterion>The runbook reproduces the whole configuration on a fresh environment.</criterion>
    <criterion>design 5.1a and spec NFR-05 state the depth that was actually required.</criterion>
    <criterion>The child-entity ownership question is answered from live metadata, with a recommendation.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <notes>Design 5.1a's privilege list is a hypothesis to test, not a spec to implement — in particular USER depth may suffice for a team that owns the records, which would be tighter than the Business Unit depth currently written down and would narrow NFR-05's exemption. The task's value is the empirical answer plus the runbook, not the clicks.

COMPLETED 2026-08-25. Full write-up: notes/task-046-secure-project-owner-role.md

The hypothesis was wrong in BOTH dimensions. Empirical minimum is ONE privilege: prvReadsprk_Project at USER (Basic) depth -- not seven at Business Unit depth. Dataverse named the one it needed when it was absent ("Principal team ... is missing prvReadsprk_Project privilege", with privilegeCount matching the role's real count, which is what proved the message was current rather than cached). Write proved unnecessary: the team is an ownership anchor and never an actor -- the BFF app user performs every mutation. Role e4ebabd9-b4a0-f111-aaac-000d3a99d1d7 is on that one team only (0 users, 0 other teams); System Administrator removed; team still memberless; assignment re-proven AFTER the removal.

ESCALATION TRIGGER 3 FIRED, in a stronger form than written. The trigger anticipated child records being owned in a reachable BU. What was found is that the SECURE PROJECT ITSELF is reachable: Test User 1 -- an ordinary non-admin in the root BU -- read a real sprk_issecure=true record owned by the secure team, because Spaarke Basic User holds prvReadsprk_Project at Deep depth and Deep at root reaches every descendant BU. This is design 5.2's unremediated blocking prerequisite, now proven end-to-end rather than inferred from a depth census. The negative control passed (a Basic-depth principal WAS denied on the same record), which is what establishes that BU containment works once no ordinary role holds Deep or Global. The fix -- BU restructure per 5.2, or narrowing Deep to Local -- was deliberately NOT applied: editing an ordinary end-user role changes every user's effective access and is an owner decision, and the constraints forbid widening or otherwise papering over a failure to make it disappear.

Two acceptance criteria are not met, both for that same external reason: "a non-admin cannot read a secure project" FAILS, and the share-then-read half of the sharing criterion is UNTESTABLE (every human with sprk_project Read holds Deep or Global, so no record exists that they cannot already read -- nothing to discriminate against). Nothing was stripped from any ordinary role.

Child-entity question answered from live relationship metadata: 18 Spaarke business entities via 19 lookups, not the 3 named in this POML; sprk_document carries TWO (sprk_project and sprk_relatedproject). Nothing assigns children to the secure team, so they are unisolated independently of the depth defect. Filed as a separate task per the step-7 instruction -- extending task 021's assign is the wrong shape, since children are created continuously long after provisioning returns.

Method note worth carrying forward: Dataverse's principal-privilege cache lags role edits by about one operation, and an early pass produced a false "assignment allowed with zero privileges". Re-probe until stable across 3+ polls and cross-check privilegeCount in any denial against the role's real count. Also: creating a role injects ~9 privileges at Global depth; AddPrivilegesRole re-injects the SharePoint four; ReplacePrivilegesRole does NOT remove them; only RemovePrivilegeRole does, one per call, with an entity-reference payload.
</task>
